  "query_preview": "..The tropical low that brought widespread rains to\nsoutheast Arizona yesterday has opened up to a short-wave \ntrough and is moving NE into northeast Arizona this morning in \nadvance of an upper low that was over northern California. Radar\nshowed isolated to scattered light showers across eastern half \nof forecast area. These showers should end from south to north \nby sunrise. With surface RH values in the 90-100 percent range, \nsome patchy fog, briefly dense, may develop before sunrise if \nthere is some clearing.\n\nFor today: Dry across most of the forecast area. The exception \nwill be near/along the AZ/NM border this afternoon with a chance\nof showers and thunderstorms exist. \n\nTonight into Friday: The short-wave trof that is currently over\ncentral Baja will lift NE across the area later tonight into\nFriday. Although the dynamics with this feature is weak, can't \nrule out isolated light showers.\n\nThis weekend through Monday: Drier air finally begins to filter\ninto southeast Arizona as flow aloft shifts more westerly. This\ncould help limit thunderstorm activity somewhat and keep \nactivity mainly east and south of Tucson. High temperatures \nreturn to at or slightly above normal.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Moisture advection out ahead of a \nsouthern stream system with bring a chance of afternoon storms \nto the eastern zones both days.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (The Sep 24 to Wed Sep 30)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 47-52% chance\nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal, 47-52% chance",
